# Web Server and Cloudflare Tunnel Setup

## Web Server Components

### Python HTTP Server ([web_server.py](mdc:web_server.py))
- Simple HTTP server for port 80
- Serves custom HTML content
- Handles connection errors gracefully
- Provides detailed error messages for port conflicts

### Port Management ([kill_port80_and_start_server.py](mdc:kill_port80_and_start_server.py))
- Automatically kills processes using port 80
- Starts Python web server on port 80
- Handles permission errors and port conflicts
- Provides fallback options

### Simplified Server ([start_port80_server.py](mdc:start_port80_server.py))
- Lightweight HTTP server implementation
- Focused on port 80 operation
- Minimal error handling for testing

## Cloudflare Tunnel Integration

### Tunnel Client ([cloudflared.exe](mdc:cloudflared.exe))
- Cloudflare tunnel executable
- Connects local port 80 to web.190801.xyz
- Runs as Windows service
- Handles SSL termination

### Service Management
- [reinstall_cloudflared.ps1](mdc:reinstall_cloudflared.ps1) - PowerShell service management
- [reinstall_cloudflared.bat](mdc:reinstall_cloudflared.bat) - Batch service management
- Requires administrator privileges for service operations

## Firewall Configuration
- [fix_firewall.ps1](mdc:fix_firewall.ps1) - PowerShell firewall rules
- [fix_firewall.bat](mdc:fix_firewall.bat) - Batch firewall rules
- Opens ports 80 and 443 for external access
- Allows Nginx and Python server traffic

## Startup Scripts
- [start_python_server_admin.bat](mdc:start_python_server_admin.bat) - Admin-privileged server start
- [start_server_simple.bat](mdc:start_server_simple.bat) - Simple server startup
- [start_port80_server.py](mdc:start_port80_server.py) - Direct server execution

## Troubleshooting
- Check port 80 availability with `netstat -ano`
- Verify firewall rules are active
- Ensure Cloudflare tunnel service is running
- Test local access before external access